Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||08-18-2000||10:05 AM||||<BR>I know if you swap a RWD 3.4 over to our cars you need to move the starter & mess with a motor mount, Is it the same if you get a 3.4 from a FWD car? I rember when my mom got ther Buick Regal the base motor was a 3.4L V6 or a series 2 3.8. If I foun a Reagal 3.4 would it just bolt it or should i just get a higher HP camaro 3.4 and modify it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||HYPTNOTiSE||08-18-2000||05:55 PM||||I'm staring a  starter drilling service for the 3.4 RWD blocks here in MD.  The service can be done with the crankshaft and engine assembled. All thats needed is a new oil pan gasket. you'd need new pistons if you get a FWD block.<P>Mike LeCompte<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||08-18-2000||07:08 PM||||New pistons!! screw that, i'll find a used camaro/firebird 3.4, all the bone yards here in frederick county i'm sure to find a few to choose from. How much will it cost to get the starter done? will I need anything else to drop in the 3.4?||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||08-19-2000||02:35 AM||||You will need a flexplate from a '88 Fiero 2.8<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||08-19-2000||07:46 AM||||Since you'd be one of my first customers, i'll do it for 100 bucks.  Ed Parks in Alabama does it for 400 i think.<P>Also you need a nuetrally balanced flywheel, from either an 88 fiero which is the lightest, or a flywheel from any front wheel drive car with a 3.1-3.4.  Ususally people use the flywheel from a DOHC 3.4 with 5 speed. You can also get an aluminum flywheel, i know a guy who makes them, they weight only like 9 lbs. <P>You'll also need a new oil pump. <P>I can also locate engines and get them at a cheaper cost usually than most, since I deal with bone yards on a daily basis. Insurance companys always have us looking for used parts to repair customers cars with.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula 18||08-19-2000||09:26 AM||||HYPTNOTiSE<BR>Hey see if you can look me up a 3.4L out of a Camaro or TA. I am looking here in NY but haven't found any yet. If could find one  you could by one drill it and then ship it to me? E-mail me and will talk. OK? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||lou_dias||08-21-2000||12:49 AM||||You have it backwards, the 88 flywheel is heavier, the DOHC 3.4 flywheel is the heaviest of all and costs less than the camaro's flywheel.<BR>About flywheels...<BR>A light flywheel will let an engine rev quicker and higher, however bottom end torque and fuel economy are sacrificed.  It's a trade-off of what you are looking for in an engine.  I used the camaro flywheel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||HYPTNOTiSE||08-21-2000||10:35 AM||||well of course i didn't mean that an 88 flywheel is lighter than an alumnum one.  Get real.  88's are cast iron and i believe weigh 14 lbs.  the DOHC flywheels weigh 2  pounds more supposedly, never weighed one.<P>||||reg||1
